            "text_KwahGE": {
              "type": "text",
              "name": "t:names.caption",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<p>PDRN (Polydeoxyribonucleotide) isn’t your typical skincare ingredient.<br/>It wasn’t discovered by beauty brands.<br/>It wasn’t invented for aesthetics.</p><p>It was originally developed in <strong>medical treatments for tissue regeneration</strong> — helping burn victims and surgery patients heal faster.</p><p>Doctors quickly noticed something unexpected: — Patients weren’t just recovering — their <strong>skin quality dramatically improved.</strong></p><ul><li>Firmer texture<br/></li><li>Faded scars<br/></li><li>Less redness and inflammation<br/></li><li>Smoother appearance — <em>almost like new skin was growing in its place</em><br/></li></ul><p>It didn’t take long for <strong>South Korean dermatologists</strong> (aka the pioneers of modern skin tech) to realize:</p><p>If PDRN could heal damaged tissue…<br/><br/><strong>could it reverse aging, too?</strong></p><p>Spoiler: <strong>Yes.</strong> And now — it’s no longer exclusive to clinics.</p>",
                "width": "fit-content",
